  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1031 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe main band of thunderstorms associated with the aforementioned\nstorm system will likely develop just west of the area late\nafternoon into early evening, sweeping eastward into early Tuesday\nmorning. These storms have a higher likelihood of being severe,\nwith wind fields and a linear storm mode supportive of mainly \ndamaging wind and leading edge tornado threats. The overall\nseverity as mentioned above is contingent on how much the\nafternoon storms impact the environment across northeast and east\ncentral Oklahoma. With an increasing low level jet and \ncorresponding uptick in low level helicity values late evening \ninto early Tuesday morning across far eastern Oklahoma and western\nArkansas, the overall threat for leading edge tornadoes may \nincrease after dark. Keeping up with the latest forecast \nexpectations into the evening and also having multiple ways to \nreceive warning information, including ways designed to wake you \nup, are both recommended actions for those that may be impacted by\nthese storms overnight. The storms should exit the western\nArkansas counties by mid to late morning Tuesday. \n\nRegarding the heavy rain and flooding potential, forecast\nprecipitable water values remain near seasonal maxes, supporting\nefficient rainfall. However, this is likely to be offset by faster\nstorm movements given the wind fields. Parts of northeast Oklahoma\nthat see multiple rounds of thunderstorms from Monday afternoon\nthrough Monday night will have the highest likelihood of seeing\nlocalized flooding impacts.\n\nThe cold front associated with the system will begin to move\nthrough early Tuesday morning, clearing the area to the east by\nTuesday afternoon. Cooler and drier conditions should prevail\nTuesday night into Wednesday night. Another fast moving storm\nsystem will move through the area Thursday, with low rain chances\nmaking a brief return to parts of northeast Oklahoma during the\nday. Overall, both high and low temperatures from the middle of \nthis week into the next weekend should remain near seasonal \nnormals, with low impact weather expected.",
